<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Lebou Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1795 CE – 1857 CE (approx.) · capital Ndakaaru</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing state of the Lebou fishing people on the Cap-Vert peninsula, ruled by councils and elders, whose capital grew into the city of Dakar.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebou</a>, fishermen and farmers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-vert">Cap-Vert</a> peninsula, were long subject to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cayor">Cayor</a>. In the 1790s they rebelled under the Muslim leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dial-diop">Dial Diop</a> and, after fighting off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor's</a> forces, established their independence around 1795, with villages such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Ndakaaru</a>, Yoff and Ngor forming a single polity.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebou</a> state had no king. Authority lay with an elected head, the Serigne <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Ndakaaru</a>, together with a council of notables and assemblies of lineage elders, an arrangement Europeans compared to a republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, spread by marabouts, gave the community cohesion, and the economy rested on fishing, farming and trade with the French post at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/goree">Goree</a> across the bay.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1857 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied the peninsula and founded the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebou-people">Lebou</a> land, and the Lebou were absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a>. They remain a recognised community in Dakar with their own traditional leadership.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lebou-republic.jpg" alt="Lebou Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: mostroneddo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dakar-Ind%C3%A9pendance.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1795 CE – 1857 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ndakaaru</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dial Diop</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wolof (Lebou)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/senegal">Senegal</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Lebou, Lebu Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1826&amp;focus=lebou-republic" aria-label="Lebou Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4203 1632 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lebou Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4523 1888L4544 1864M4523 1888L4550 1838"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4544 1864h0M4550 1838h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4523 1888h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4523 1888h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1826&amp;focus=lebou-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
